WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.526
Can Parliament change any part of the

2
00:00:02.526 --> 00:00:04.151
Constitution?

3
00:00:04.151 --> 00:00:06.345
In 1973, the Supreme Court gave an

4
00:00:06.345 --> 00:00:08.073
answer that still holds.

5
00:00:08.473 --> 00:00:10.574
Parliament can amend the Constitution under Article

6
00:00:10.574 --> 00:00:11.884
368.

7
00:00:12.285 --> 00:00:14.723
Kesavananda Bharati, head of a mutt in

8
00:00:14.723 --> 00:00:17.846
Kerala, went to court against state laws

9
00:00:17.846 --> 00:00:20.203
that took over the mutt's land.

10
00:00:20.203 --> 00:00:22.802
It grew into a much bigger question.

11
00:00:23.202 --> 00:00:24.688
Thirteen judges heard it,

12
00:00:24.688 --> 00:00:27.184
the largest bench in the court's history.

13
00:00:27.184 --> 00:00:29.725
The hearing ran for sixty-eight days.

14
00:00:30.124 --> 00:00:31.448
On the twenty-fourth of April,

15
00:00:31.448 --> 00:00:33.003
1973, they decided,

16
00:00:33.003 --> 00:00:34.789
by seven votes to six.

17
00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:36.873
Parliament can amend any part of the

18
00:00:36.873 --> 00:00:38.232
Constitution.

19
00:00:38.232 --> 00:00:40.923
But it cannot destroy its basic structure.

20
00:00:41.323 --> 00:00:43.285
What counts as basic structure?

21
00:00:43.285 --> 00:00:45.804
Courts have named the supremacy of the

22
00:00:45.804 --> 00:00:47.244
Constitution, judicial review,

23
00:00:47.244 --> 00:00:48.881
federalism and secularism,

24
00:00:48.881 --> 00:00:50.214
among others.

25
00:00:50.615 --> 00:00:52.380
For Mains: name the case,

26
00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:53.912
cite Article 368,

27
00:00:53.912 --> 00:00:56.302
and explain basic structure in one line.

